          "version>=": "1.0.2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "vulkan": {
      "description": "Create Vulkan targets",
      "$comment": "On Android vulkan-loader is not built: the loader is the OS's libvulkan (NDK link library, never a built port). directx-dxc is a host tool for the test-shader step on every configuration that builds tests; it must not set platform:windows, because vcpkg evaluates that expression against the target triplet and would drop the host tool from the Android closure.",
      "supports": "(windows & x64) | android",
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "name": "directx-dxc",
          "host": true,
          "version>=": "2023-03-01#2"
        },
        {
          "name": "vulkan-loader",
          "platform": "windows",
          "version>=": "1.4.304.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "with-granny": {
      "description": "Include Granny support",
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "name": "granny",
          "version>=": "2.12.0.14"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "overrides": [
    {
      "name": "protobuf",
      "version": "3.6.0.1"
    },
    {
      "name": "libpng",
      "version": "1.6.47"
    },
    {
      "name": "openexr",
      "version": "3.2.4#0"
    },
    {
      "name": "libsquish",
      "version": "1.15#14"
    },
    {
      "$comment": "vulkan-headers, vulkan-loader and vulkan-memory-allocator are a matched SDK set and must move together: the loader's generated VulkanLoaderConfig.cmake hard-references the Vulkan::Headers target of its own release, so pinning one and leaving the others on a bare version>= lets a baseline move break the configure in whichever direction the unpinned side drifts (observed once already, headers pinned / loader floating -- see docs/spikes/M4-phase2a-test-harness.md \u00c2\u00a74b).",
      "name": "vulkan-headers",
      "version": "1.4.304.1#1"
    },
    {
      "name": "vulkan-loader",
      "version": "1.4.304.1"
    },
    {
      "name": "vulkan-memory-allocator",
      "version": "3.3.0"
    }
  ],
  "$comment-android": "The Android tree is configured only on POSIX hosts. carbon-blue depends on python3, and CPython cannot be cross-built from Windows (its pybuilddir step runs a POSIX-only sysconfig on the build python); a POSIX-built cache entry cannot be restored on Windows either, because vcpkg's ABI hash embeds the host (docs/spikes/artifacts/m6-python3-abi-probe-20260901.txt, four causes). So there are no android platform gates here: the engine's whole dependency set is in the Android closure, and Windows is not an Android build host (M6 second-half spec D3). amd-fidelityfx-cas and -cacao are header-only copies and the engine cannot compile without them (Tr2SSAO.cpp includes ffx_cacao.cpp) -- the first-half note that they never lift was mistaken about what they are."
}
